      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">The Two Dominant Panel Formats: 2×2 m vs 2.4×1.2 m</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        These two formats exist because of their destination markets, and they are not interchangeable:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>2.0 × 2.0 m</strong> is the European fence-panel format (roughly matching historic 6 ft 7 in norms). It pairs with 50×50 mm, 50×100 mm, 75×75 mm, and 100×100 mm apertures, wire diameters of 3.0–5.0 mm, and usually a bent edge (V-fold or U-fold) for rigid <a href="/products/wire-mesh-fence-panels/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">fence panels</a>. Because the panel is close to square, it stacks and racks efficiently and suits automated fencing lines in Europe.</li>
        <li><strong>2.4 × 1.2 m</strong> is the UK-derived format (8 ft × 4 ft exactly) used for construction mesh, BRC-style reinforcement, and general-purpose panels. It matches UK pallet and rebar-mesh handling conventions, and 2.4 m aligns with common bay widths in temporary fencing and concrete work.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Which should you order? If your panels bolt into an existing European fence system, match that system—post spacing, clamp positions, and fold geometry are designed around 2.0 m widths. If you are pouring concrete or building your own frames, 2.4 × 1.2 m usually gives better coverage per panel and slightly better price per m² because the factory wastes less edge trim. For sheer material efficiency, a 2.5 × 2.0 m "jumbo" panel is increasingly common from larger Hebei lines and offers the lowest cost per m² on volume orders.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Pro Tip</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Panel price per m² falls as panel area rises—one 2.4 × 1.2 m panel has roughly the same handling cost as one 1.2 × 1.0 m panel but covers double the area. If your installation allows it, always quote the largest panel the factory's line can produce (usually 2.5 × 3.0 m for mesh-only panels, larger on request).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

