    <!-- Imperial -->
    <section id="imperial" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Imperial-Cut Sizes: Why Hebei Factories Make Both</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Buyers sometimes assume a Chinese factory will quote metric only and then struggle to match imperial drawings. In practice, the opposite problem is more common: Anping lines run imperial conversions all day. The UK's 8 ft × 4 ft sheet is cut as 2.44 × 1.22 m (not 2.4 × 1.2—that is the rounded metric equivalent), US "50 ft rolls" ship as 15.24 m, and "3 ft wide" arrives as 0.914 m. Wire diameters follow the same pattern: a "4 mm" European panel and a "6 gauge" US panel are close cousins, and factories switch between metric mm and AWG/SWG gauge charts fluently. If your drawings are imperial, send them as-is and add one line: "Dimensions in feet/inches; confirm factory will cut to exact imperial, not rounded metric."
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        One genuine trap: rounding. If your drawing says 8 ft and the factory's machine is programmed in meters, 8 ft becomes 2.44 m—but if someone lazily enters 2.4 m, you lose 40 mm per panel. Across 500 panels that is 20 m of missing fence. The fix costs one sentence in the PO: "Exact size 2440 × 1220 mm; ±5 mm tolerance; rounded-metric equivalents not accepted."
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        If you are converting between gauge systems yourself, our <a href="/tools/wire-gauge-conversion/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">wire gauge conversion tool</a> maps AWG, SWG, and millimeters, and the <a href="/charts/mesh-size-chart/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">mesh size chart</a> covers aperture conversions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Custom -->
    <section id="custom" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Customization and Factory Tolerances</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        "Custom size" in Anping almost never carries a tooling charge—panels are cut and folded to length on the same line. What it does affect is lead time (add 3–7 days for non-stock formats) and sometimes price per m² on small orders, because the factory optimizes material yield around its standard formats.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
