    <!-- Loading -->
    <section id="loading" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Panel Size and Container Loading</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Panels are the least efficient mesh product to ship: rigid, flat, and mostly air. A 20 ft container's door opening is about 2.34 m high × 2.28 m wide, so panels longer than ~2.3 m must load through the open-top or be angled—2.4 × 1.2 m panels fit diagonally but waste volume, and 2.5 × 3.0 m jumbo panels typically require flat-rack or open-top containers with the price premium that brings. As a rule, keep panel length under 2.3 m if you want cheap 20 ft FCL loading.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Stacking also matters: panels are usually bundled 10–25 per steel pallet. A standard 2.4 × 1.2 m bundle stack gives you roughly 55–70 bundles in a 20 ft container depending on wire diameter—model your exact order in the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">container loading calculator</a> and send the loading plan to the factory so they band and pack to it.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Rule of Thumb</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          If panels are over 40% of your order value, get quotes for both your ideal size and the nearest stock size. Stock 2.0 × 2.0 m or 2.4 × 1.2 m panels ship in days from inventory; custom sizes add a week of production and sometimes a per-panel surcharge under 300 panels.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the most common welded wire mesh panel size?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Globally, the 2.0 × 2.0 m fence panel and the 2.4 × 1.2 m (8 ft × 4 ft) general-purpose panel dominate exports from Hebei. For construction reinforcement mesh, 2.4 × 6.0 m sheets are standard in some markets, but that length requires open-top loading. If you name either 2×2 m or 2.4×1.2 m, virtually any Anping factory can quote from stock.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can Hebei factories cut exact imperial sizes like 8 ft × 4 ft?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes—factories serving UK, US, and Australian buyers cut 2.44 × 1.22 m (8×4 ft), 1.83 × 1.22 m (6×4 ft), and 0.91 m (3 ft) widths daily. The risk is rounding to 2.4 × 1.2 m. Put "exact imperial cut 2440 × 1220 mm, rounded metric not accepted" in writing and verify a sample with a tape measure before production.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What tolerance should I allow on panel dimensions?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            ±5 mm on length and width is the industry default and fine for most construction and fencing. Tighten to ±3 mm if panels must align with posts, racking, or machine frames. Wire diameter tolerance is ±5% under ASTM A974—combine that with a weight check using the <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">weight calculator</a> to catch undersized wire.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is there a minimum order for custom panel sizes?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Not formally—Anping lines cut to order. Practically, under about 100–300 panels factories add a small setup charge or fold the extra labor into a higher per-panel price. On one full container (roughly 500+ standard panels), custom dimensions normally cost the same as stock sizes.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Rolls or panels—which is cheaper to import?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Rolls win on freight: they compress and load densely, so you ship more steel per container. Panels win on installation labor: no on-site cutting, straighter lines, and bent edges add rigidity. For fence projects over a few hundred meters, panels usually have the lower total installed cost despite higher product price—run both options through the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">container loading calculator</a> to see the freight difference.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

